## COMMUNICATION
Innovative techniques have been developed to address energetic and environmental issues in ocean.[1l The great potential of marine economy and related research activities prompts rapid development of energy supply systems for underwater devices.[2] To support adequate energy for the devices during long-term operation under sea, generating energy in seawater directly is an essential strategy.[3] Differ from many of the seawater energy harvesting devices, electrochemical devices can supply stable electricity thus have been widely utilized.[4] By either using seawater as electrolytes or electron acceptors, underwater appliances have been driven successfully.[5] However, to adapt to the extreme and complex conditions, a seawater electrochemical system which can automatically switching between high energy and power modes is highly needed. In this case, the underwater devices can not only work for a very long term, but also can move fast or afford other loads with high power request during a short term. However, building up such a system is still a big challenge so far.
Marine organisms solve this challenge via cellular respiration Depending on the required output power, the respiration process can be switched between anaerobic and aerobic by choosing various electron acceptors,[6] making high power and high energy supply to be possible in one organism. Here we describe a cellular respiration inspired dual-mode energy extraction from seawater by using nanoporous frameworks to initiatively select electron acceptors depending on power output.
Our design takes advantages of multivalent coordination frameworks which are assembled from organic ligands and redoxable metal-containing nodes.[7l When electrons are injected, the coordination frameworks can either transfer the electrons to other acceptors like dissolved-oxygen,[8] or store the electrons upon insertion of alkali metal ions.[9] Depending on the electron acceptance pathway, the system works in dual modes. In the high-energy working mode (aerobic respiration analog)， the system use the dissolved-oxygen molecules are the electron acceptor. Because the dissolved oxygen is inexhaustible in seawater,[10] energy supply in this mode can be very high as far as enough electrons can be injected (Fig. 1). However, due to the limited concentration of the dissolved oxygen, the output power can not be high. In the high-power mode (anaerobic respiration analog), although the alkali metal ions do not accept electrons directly， their intercalation can induce accommodation of electrons into the coordination frameworks to neutralize the charge, forming electric flow (Fig. 1). Because of the abundance of the alkali metal ions in the seawater,[11] a great number of electrons can be accepted by the coordination frameworks, leading to a high theoretic power output. Thereby, if we can find the coordination frameworks which are robust enough to endure rapid and repeatable accommodation of alkali ions rapidly against the corrosion of seawater, a dual-mode energy extraction device can be realized.
We select Prussian Blue, a type of metal-cyanide coordination frameworks with an open perovskite structure consisting of $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}/\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ nodes  and cyanide  bridges.[12]  The Prussian Blue nanoparticles were synthesized by a kineticcontrolled crystallization method with average-size of around 40 nm as shown in Fig. S1a and S1b.[13] Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns of the samples show a pure phase of facecentered cubic structure $(F m{\bar{3}}m)$ (Fig. S2).[14] The structure of the Prussian Blue nanoparticles is rigid upon accommodation of alkali metal ions as demonstrated by PXRD. The crystal structure was kept to be $F m\bar{3}m$ before and after insertion of ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ ions with slight lattice swelling. (Fig. S3).
## COMMUNICATION
Auto-switchable dual-mode seawater energy extraction system
This Prussian Blue cathode was collected with a metal anode such as Al, Mg and Zn (Fig. 2a) to assemble an electrochemical device. Steady current flow with an output voltage of $\mathcal{1}.0\ V$ was generated instantly by dipping both electrodes into simulated seawater (NaCl concentration of $3.5\mathrm{wt.}\%\$ ）(Fig. 2b). The voltage was close to $1\vee$ when the output power was $0.01\mathrm{\mW\cm^{-2}}$ (high energy mode). The voltage of the continuous electric flow was stable for a long term while keeping the output power to be constant. When the output power was increased to $5\mathsf{m}\mathsf{W}\mathsf{c m}^{-2};$ the voltage dropt to $0.85\vee$ (high power mode). The voltage again returned back to be more than $0.9\mathrm{V}$ once the output power was decreased to $0.01\mathrm{\mW\cm^{-2}}$ . Such an autoswitching of working modes was observed in the seawater which was collected from the Donghai sea near Ningbo city in China. Fig. S4 presents that the working voltage of the system was swtiched upon the change of the output power.
The voltage changes during switching of the output power density correlate to different electron transferring/storing pathway. To verify the mechanism for the two working modes, several samples were taken at different time for subsequent characterizations. The points were marked as A,B, C and D in the curve, representing the reaction at 800 s, 33000 s, 35400 s, and 35800 s, respectively (Fig. 2b). The low $\mathsf{F e}^{\mathsf{I I}}$ ratio at points A and B indicates that the electrons were transferred to dissolved oxygen with a low output power, not stored inside the frameworks (Fig. 2c, Fig. S5-s8). Alternation of the ratio of $\mathsf{F e}^{\mathsf{I I}}$ to Fe in the samples was at point C, implying that electrons have been stored in the frameworks when high power mode was on $(5\mathsf{m}\mathsf{W}\mathsf{c m}^{-2},$ Reduction of the $\mathsf{F e}^{\mathsf{I I}}$ ratio at point D suggested that the electrons stored in the frameworks were released at a low output power $(0.01\mathrm{mW}\mathsf{c m}^{-2})$ . The Na content in the samples also supports this observation. From point A to B, the contents of Na were almost the same, suggesting that the injected electrons were transferred to the dissolved oxygen in seawater. The Na content of sample C was increased from $8.4\mathrm{wt.}\%$ to $14.5\mathrm{wt.}\%$ in the high power mode $(5\mathrm{\mw\cm^{-2}})$ ，indicating the insertion of $\mathsf{N a}^{+}$ ions which was accompanied with reduction of the $\mathsf{F e}^{\mathsf{I I I}}$ in the frameworks.[15] For the sample at point D, the Na contents was decreased again, matching with the re-oxidization of $\mathsf{F e}^{\mathsf{I I}}$ . To get deep insight about the high-power working mode, ex-situ UV spectra were shown in Fig. S9. With a large number of electrons injected, the absorption bands of $\mathsf{F e}^{\mathsf{I I I\_C N\_F e}^{\mathsf{I I}}}$ at around $700\mathsf{n m}$ gradually disappeared (Fig. S9),[15]  suggesting that the $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ was reduced to $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ Besides, the cathodic peak at around $0.05\mathrm{V}$ (vs Ag/AgCl) in the cyclic voltammetry (CV) curves also implies that reduction of $\mathsf{F e}^{\mathsf{I I I}}$ to $\mathsf{F e}^{\mathsf{I I}}$ happened during the high power mode (Fig. S10).[16] All the results confirm that electrons are transferred to the dissolved oxygen in high energy mode, and stored in the frameworks upon insertion of ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ ions in the high power mode.
The mechanism for autoswitching of the working modes is the difference in reaction potential and concentrations of the reactants. The potential of the $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ reaction (around 0.9 V)is higher than the intercalation reaction of Prussian Blue (around $0.7\ \mathrm{V}$ 。 The electrons tend to be accepted by the dissolved $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ rather than the Prussian blue. Therefore,when the output power is low, the electrons go to the dissolved $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ .When the outputpower becomes high, there is not enough $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ to accept the electrons because of the low concentration of $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ dissolved in seawater. Therefore, the electrons have to go to Prussian Blue, causing intercalation of ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ ions. When the output power decreases again, the electrons go to the dissolved $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ again.
## COMMUNICATION
Influence of environmental factors on this dual mode system was shown in Fig. 3. As mentioned above, the oxygen plays an important role in high energy mode. In the absence of dissolved oxygen, electric flow soon got vanished in the high energy mode (output power was $0.01\mathrm{\mW\cm^{-2}},$ as shown in Fig.3a. This result is a direct evidence that the electrons were accepted by the dissolved oxygen when the system works in a high energy mode. The concentration of $\mathsf{N a}^{+}$ ions affects the maximum output power in the high power working mode as illustrated in Fig. 3b. By decreasing the concentration of NaCl, the maximum output power was decreased significantly. The reason is because that only with enough ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ ions for insertion, the output power can be increased when the system is operated in a high power mode. Because seawater is very corrosive, the stability of the Prussian Blue crystals needs to be investigated. After immersed the whole system in seawater (from the Donghai sea near Ningbo city in China) for one month (Fig. 3c),the Prussian Blue crystals maintained the face-centered cubic structure $(F m{\bar{3}}m)$ without forming any impurities. The system still could deliver an output power of $5\mathsf{m}\mathsf{W}\mathsf{c m}^{-2}\mathsf{i}$ in the high power mode (Fig. S11). The dualmode seawater energy extraction system displayed steady cycling performance upon switching of the working modes between the high energy mode $(0.01\mathrm{\mW\cm^{-2}}.$ ）and highpower mode $(5\mathsf{m}\mathsf{W}\mathsf{c m}^{-2})$ for 100 times (Fig.3d). Our system could be refreshed in an open seawater environment. The dissolved $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ can oxidize the Prussian Blue electrode to realse the residual sodium ions. After that, the voltage of the system was changed back (Fig. S12).
We explored the potential of this system by using various metals as the anodes (Fig. S13-18). The maxima output power density was achieved to be $100{\pm}4~\mathsf{m w}~\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ by using Mg as the anodes when the system was working at the high power mode. The switchable working modes make this system to be very competitive. Comparison of this dual-mode system with the other existing seawater electrochemical devices illustrates that this is the only case which can supply high energy (3960 Wh $\mathsf{k g}^{-1}$ in theory) and high power $100\pm4\mathrm{\mW\cm^{-2}}$ in practical) in one system (Fig. 4a and Table S1).[4.17] Such a system allows us to drive devices by using seawater (from the Donghai sea near Ningbo city in China). The system worked under the high energy mode continuously for 4 days without suffering any power loss (Fig. 4b).A red light-emitting diode (LED)was lighted up after working for 4 days (Fig. S19). In the high power mode, the output power permitted us to light up 39 led lights and drove propeller.
In conclusion, we reported a dual-mode seawater energy extraction system by mimicking the cellular respiration of marine organisms. We demonstrated that Prussian Blue nanoparticles interacted with various solutes in seawater, making high energy and power supply to be possible in one system. The system selectively transferred the electrons to the dissolved oxygen or stored the electrons in the frameworks with the help of intercalation of alkali metal ions in seawater. Because of the different concentration of the solutes, the working mode switched between a high energy mode (dissolved-oxygen route) and a high power output mode (alkali-metal ions way). The large theoretical energy density $(3960\mathsf{\ w h\ k g^{-1}})$ ：and high instantaneous power density $(100\pm4\mathsf{m w}\mathsf{c m}^{-2},$ ）with impressive stability in seawater make the system to be a possible candidate for practical applications in seawater.
